{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791704669,"nonce":"flbfuv3yD8dL91sGa5sfpGcpfz4=","title":"Hot reload replaces a class's field and method lists while other threads may read them","message":"A reload replaces the C field list and prototype list of a class (c_types.rs set_class_fields, set_class_protos) and then rebuilds its runtime object (hlp_rebuild_obj_proto) while Haxe threads, fibers and compile threads may be running. The two lists are published with a count and an array pointer written one after the other, and the runtime object afterwards, with no lock the readers take.\n\nFound by the read-only review of the field slice (5bfcbb0). The sound parts: every allocation of an object is at least the new layout size, the interpreter and both tiers bound field reads by the runtime object's nfields, and the lists only grow with the old entries first. What is left:\n\n- A reader on ARM64 that loads the count and the array pointer without an acquire can see the new count with the old, shorter array (the writer now issues a release fence between the two stores, but plain loads do not order against it).\n- build_obj_rt reads nfields and fields under the GC lock and HL_GLOBAL_LOCK; set_class_fields takes neither, so a first-time build on another thread can see a torn pair.\n- hlp_obj_fields and hlp_type_instance_fields no longer write past the array they allocate, but the array can still be sized from the old runtime object and filled from the new list, so a Reflect.fields call that races a reload can return a shorter or incomplete list.\n\nA way to close it without making every reader lock: give each class list spare capacity when reload is enabled, append in place, and publish the new count with a release store that readers pair with an acquire load. A reload then never moves an array a reader holds. The alternative is one std entry that replaces the lists and rebuilds the runtime object under gc_guard and HL_GLOBAL_LOCK, which fixes build_obj_rt but not the lock-free readers.\n\nExposure is small: another thread has to read that class in the microseconds a reload takes. ashui's reloads run on the UI thread.","files":null}]}